# Untitled

## Hydrophobic Core Malleability of a De Novo Designed Three-helix Bundle Protein
<sup>1</sup>The Johnson Research Foundation, Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics University of Pennsylvania Philadelphia, PA 19104-6059, USA
<sup>2</sup>Institute of Cell Biophysics Pushchino, Moscow, 142290
De novo protein design provides a tool for testing the principles that stabilize the structures of proteins. Recently, we described the design and structure determination of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), a three-helix bundle protein with a well-packed hydrophobic core. Here, we test the malleability and adaptability of this protein's structure by mutating a small, Ala residue (A60) in its core to larger, hydrophobic side-chains, Leu and Ile. Such changes introduce strain into the structures of natural proteins, and therefore generally destabilize the native state. By contrast, these mutations were slightly stabilizing (\(\sim1.5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\)) to the tertiary structure of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\). The value of \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) for unfolding of these mutants was not greatly affected relative to wild-type, indicating that the change in solvent accessibility for unfolding was similar. However, two-dimensional heteronuclear single quantum coherence spectra indicate that the protein adjusts to the introduction of steric bulk in different ways. A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) showed serious erosion in the dispersion of both the amide backbone as well as the side-chain methyl chemical shifts. By contrast, A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) showed excellent dispersion of the backbone resonances, and selective changes in dispersion of the aliphatic side-chains proximal to the site of mutation. Together, these data suggest that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), although folded into a unique three-dimensional structure, is nevertheless more malleable and flexible than most natural, native proteins.
Keywords: conformational specificity; de novo protein design; protein folding; thermodynamics; three-helix bundle protein
## Introduction
Although considerable progress has been made in de novo protein design (for a review, see DeGrado et al., 1999), understanding how to design large globular proteins from scratch with native-like characteristics still remains a challenge. The design of well-packed hydrophobic cores has been one of the major stumbling blocks (Bryson et al., 1995). Re-packing of hydrophobic cores using computational re-packing algorithms have made tremendous strides and now even allow for backbone movement during optimization (Dahiyat et al., 1997; Dahiyat & Mayo, 1997a,b; Harbury et al., 1995; Lazar et al., 1997; Street & Mayo, 1998; Su & Mayo, 1997).
1997; Dahiyat & Mayo, 1997a,b; Harbury et al., 1995; Lazar et al., 1997; Street & Mayo, 1998; Su & Mayo, 1997).
The role of conformational specificity in the sidechain packing of hydrophobic cores of natural proteins has been studied intensely for decades (Kellis et al., 1988; Lim & Sauer, 1991; Matthews, 1995; Richards & Lim, 1993; Shortle, 1992). Here, conformational specificity refers to the ability of a protein sequence to confer a unique tertiary fold with a well-packed hydrophobic core, a hallmark feature of natural proteins. Early attempts in de novo protein design usually resulted in molten globules with loosely packed hydrophobic cores (Bryson et al., 1995). De novo design of proteins with well-packed apolar cores has now been accomplished for coiled coils (Harbury et al., 1998; Ogihara et al., 1997), a few \(\alpha\)-helical bundles (Schafmeister et al., 1997, 1993; Jing et al., 1997; Brive et al., 1997; Bryson et al., 1998; Hill & DeGrado, 1998; Johansson et al., 1998), zinc-finger proteins
(Dahiyat & Mayo, 1997a; Pomerantz et al., 1998; Struthers et al., 1996), and other small globular proteins (Gu et al., 1995; Lazar et al., 1997; Malakauskas & Mayo, 1998; Riddle et al., 1997). The majority of the successful designed proteins with well-packed hydrophobic cores used some form of computational re-packing algorithm (Bryson et al., 1998; Dahiyat & Mayo, 1997a; Harbury et al., 1998; Lazar et al., 1997; Malakauskas & Mayo, 1998). A general theme emerging from hydrophobic core re-packing studies of de novo designed proteins involves the incorporation of several \(\upbeta\)-branched and aromatic residues. Incorporation of \(\upbeta\)-branched and aromatic amino acid residues have restricted mobility (Brive et al., 1997; Bryson et al., 1998) because they generally adopt one \(\chi^{1}\) rotamer state in \(\alpha\)-helices (Dunbrack & Cohen, 1997; Lovell et al., 2000; Stapley & Doig, 1997). This restriction in turn, places constraints on the remaining apolar side-chains. In recent work, we described the design (Bryson et al., 1998) and solution structure determination (Walsh et al., 1999) of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), a synthetic three-helix bundle protein. This protein was synthesized, beginning with the structure of a homotrimeric antiparallel three-stranded coiled coil peptide. The core of this trimer was composed of Leu side-chains, and although it had a well-defined crystal structure (Lovejoy et al., 1993), it adopted multiple conformations in solution (Bryson et al., 1998). A side-chain re-packing algorithm was used to define a more diverse set of residues and loops were built between individual helices. The NMR solution structure of the resulting protein, \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), was well-defined, and in very good agreement with the design (Walsh et al., 1999). Further, its thermodynamic properties and hydrogen exchange closely resembled those of natural proteins (Walsh et al., 1999). These data indicated that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) was fully native-like, which is of significance to the understanding of protein folding as well as the design of novel, folded proteins. However, it is also important to examine the dynamic properties of designed proteins. For example, the structures of designed proteins to date vary considerably in their resolution and definition of their side-chain conformations. For example, \(75\,\%\) of the core residues in \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) showed unique \(\chi^{1}\) angles, versus \(100\,\%\) for a designed four-helix bundle protein (Hill & DeGrado, 1998). Do these differences reflect in dynamic properties of the proteins, or do they reflect differences in the number of resolved NOEs, and details relating to structure determination?
To address this question, we are measuring the dynamics of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) using NMR relaxation methods (unpublished). Further, in the current manuscript, we examine the response of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) to small-to-large mutations of Ala60 to Leu or Ile in the core of the protein. Such mutations in natural proteins are often tolerated, but are generally destabilizing by 1.5 to \(5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol}^{-1}\), depending on the malleability of the site (Liu et al., 2000). Therefore, the thermodynamic and spectroscopic properties of these
mutants provide information concerning the flexibility of the core of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\).
## Results
## Aggregation states
The aggregation states of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) were determined by sedimentation equilibrium experiments. The equilibrium curves for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) were run at three speeds of 30,000, 35,000, and 40,000 RPM (Figure 1). As expected, global fitting the curves to a single monomeric species gives molecular masses within \(4\,\%\) of the theoretical molecular mass. The experimental molecular weights of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (theoretical MW of 8187 Da), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (theoretical MW of 8229 Da), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (theoretical MW of 8229 Da) have values of 7873 \((\pm\,36)\) Da, 8580 \((\pm\,26)\) Da, and 8516 \((\pm\,20)\) Da, respectively. The theoretical molecular weights are higher than published protein sequences (Walsh et al., 1999) due to the use of uniformly \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\)-labeled protein. There was no improvement in fitting the curves to higher order aggregation states. Furthermore, the NMR linewidths of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are sharp typical of a 73 amino acid protein. Even at millimolar concentrations, there is no evidence of any broad conformational exchange peaks indicative of aggregation.
## Fluorescence spectroscopy
The environment of the W4 side-chain of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) was investigated by fluorescence measurements. All experiments were carried out under similar conditions to the NMR solution structure (Walsh et al., 1999), and the NMR relaxation studies (unpublished data) with protein concentrations of \(1\;\mathrm{mM}\) in \(50\;\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}\ 5.0)\), at \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The fluorescence emission spectra for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) all have a maximum at \(343\;\mathrm{nm}\). Therefore, the W4 side-chain of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and variants experience predominantly a polar environment. This is corroborated by examination of the solution structure of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (Walsh et al., 1999). The indole \(\mathrm{N}\varepsilon\mathrm{H}\) group of W4 is pointing out into solution and is \(\sim33\,\%\) solvent exposed. Also, the W4 side-chain adopts two \(\chi^{1}\) rotamers in the family of solution structures of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (Walsh et al., 1999). All three variants also have a polarization degree of 0.22. Tryptophan lifetime measurements were conducted to obtain estimates of the overall rotational correlation times, \(\tau_{\mathrm{c}}\). The lifetime decays fit better to a double exponential rather than a mono-exponential. The \(\tau_{\mathrm{c}}\) times were 3.3, 3.2, and 2.7 ns for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), A60L-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and A60I-\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), respectively with an accuracy of 0.2 ns. \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) NMR relaxation studies of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) yield a longer \(\tau_{\mathrm{c}}\) time of 5.2 ns under similar experimental conditions (unpublished data). The trend of shorter derived \(\tau_{\mathrm{c}}\) values determined from fluorescence measurements relative to NMR relaxation methods
have been seen previously for a zinc-finger peptide (Palmer et al., 1993), thioredoxin (Kemple et al., 1994), lysozyme (Buck et al., 1995), and parvalbumin (Moncrieffe et al., 2000).
In summary, the fluorescence properties of the mutants were identical to those of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\). This iden
tity indicates that the Trp residue in each protein, which is distant from the site of mutation, experiences a similar overall environment and has similar dynamic behavior on the nsec time scale. This finding argues against any large-scale changes in the overall dynamic properties of the protein.
## Thermodynamic stability
\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are all \(\alpha\) -helical as judged by far UV CD spectroscopy. The spectra show the distinctive double minima at 208 and \(222\;\mathrm{nm}\) as well a the maximum at \(192\;\mathrm{nm}\) peak characteristic of the helical conformation (data not shown). The guanidine hydrochloride \((\mathrm{GdmCl})\) denaturations of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) all lost their \(\uptheta_{222}\) in a single, cooperative transition (Figure 2). The denaturation curves fit well to a two-state unfolding transition \((\mathrm{N}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{U})\) expressed by the following equation (Santoro & Bolen, 1988, 1992):
\[
\Delta G_{\mathrm{obs}}=\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}+m[GdmCl]
\]
\[\Delta G_{\mathrm{obs}}=\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}+m[G d m C l]\]where \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{obs}}\) is the free energy change for unfolding at a given molarity of GdmCl, \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) is the free energy for unfolding extrapolated to \(0\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{GdmCl},\) \(m\) is the change in \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{obs}}\) with respect to the concentration of GdmCl, and [GdmCl] is the concentration of GdmCl. The GdmCl denaturation parameters for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are listed in Table 1. \(\Delta\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) was calculated using the method described by Fersht (1999), utilizing the \(m\) and \(C_{\mathrm{m}}\) values. A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are more stable than \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) by 1.71 and \(1.55\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) , respectively (Table 1). The \(\Delta\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) values for A60L\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) show that small-to-large mutations in the core of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are stabilizing; where as, alanine to leucine/isoleucine mutations in the core of T4 lysozyme were all destabilizing by 1.5 to \(4.5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol}^{-1}\) (Liu et al., 2000).
\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) have GdmCl denaturation parameters similar to natural occurring three-helix bundle proteins of similar size (Schneider et al., 1998). For example, the \(Z\) -domain of protein A has a \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}},\) \(m_{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) and \(C_{\mathrm{m}}\) values of \(6.6\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1},\) , \(1.7\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\;\;\mathrm{M}^{-1},\) , and \(3.9\;\mathrm{M},\) respectively (Cedergren et al., 1993). \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) have higher \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) and \(C_{\mathrm{m}}\) values relative to another de novo designed three-helix bundle (c3-1). c3-1 has a \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) of \(4.6\;\mathrm{kcal}\) \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) and a \(C_{\mathrm{m}}\) of \(2.1\;\mathrm{M}\) (Johansson et al., 1998). Similar to the \(\alpha_{3}\) family (Bryson et al., 1998), the \(\alpha3{-}1\) three-helix bundle protein by Dutton and coworkers also used as the starting point the crystal structure of the three-stranded coiled-coil, Coil-Ser (Lovejoy et al., 1993). However, the hydrophobic cores are completely different between \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\alpha3{-}1\) (Johansson et al., 1998; Walsh et al., 1999).
Mutations of the original design (c3-1) changing the buried C51V and introducing a tyrosine for W32 (named \(\alpha3\mathrm{W}^{1}\) and \(\alpha3\mathrm{Y}^{1},\) , respectively) have \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{U}}\) and \(C_{\mathrm{m}}\) values of \(6.2\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) and \(2.2\;\mathrm{M},\) and \(5.4\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) and \(1.9\;\mathrm{M},\) respectively similar to \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) . Both \(\alpha3\mathrm{W}^{1}\) and \(\alpha3\mathrm{Y}^{1}\) have \(m\) values of \(2.8\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\;\mathrm{M}^{-1}\) .
The \(m\) value determined from the GdmCl denatuation has been attributed to an increase in accessible surface area upon unfolding. The three variants have \(m\) values of 2.41, 2.21, and \(2.15\;\mathrm{kcal}\) \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\;\;\mathrm{M}^{-1}\) for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) respectively. All three variants unfold to the same unfolding baseline in GdmCl by CD spectroscopy (at \(222\;\mathrm{nm},\) , data not shown) suggestive of the same unfolded, random coil structure. Therefore, all variants show a similar change in the accessibility of the side-chains to GdmCl between the native and unfolded state.
Thermal denaturations were measured by monitoring \([\theta]_{222}\) of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) A60L- \(\cdot\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) and \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (Figure 3). The variants of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) showed high thermal stability with midpoints between 85 to \(95\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) over a \(\mathrm{pH}\) range from 3.0 to 7.0, which is typical of small globular proteins (Alexander et al., 1992). To obtain an estimate of \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) for unfolding the thermal denaturations were repeated at various GdmCl concentrations, and the data analyzed
using the Gibbs-Helmoltz equation:
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\Delta G(T)=\Delta H_{\mathrm{m}}(1-(T/T_{\mathrm{m}}))}\\&{\qquad+\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}(T-T_{\mathrm{m}}-T\ln(T/T_{\mathrm{m}}))}\end{array}
\]
where \(\Delta G(T)\) is the free energy change at a given temperature, \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{m}}\) is the enthalpy change at the thermal midpoint transition \(\left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right)\) where \(\Delta G(T)=0\),
and \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) is the heat capacity change for unfolding. The folded and unfolded baselines, \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{m}},\) and \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\) were fit locally for each individual GdmCl concentration. \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) was globally fit over the entire range of GdmCl concentration for each variant. The thermodynamic parameters \((\Delta H_{\mathrm{m}},\ T_{\mathrm{m}},\) and \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}})\) for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D},\) \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are listed in Table 2. The values of \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{m}}\) and \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) were
determined for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) with confidence where both hot and cold denaturation occurred (Figure 3).
The \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) determined from the Gibbs-Helmoltz method (equation 2) has a value of \(683\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) while the van't Hoff analysis has a value of \(878\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\). The mean of the two methods yields a \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) of \(781\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) which gives a \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) value on a per residue basis of \(10.8\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\). The values of \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) for A60L\(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) using the Gibbs-Helmoltz method have values of \(703\) and \(725\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\), respectively. Using van't Hoff analysis, the \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) of \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) have values of \(879\) and \(999\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\), respectively. The average value for \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has a value of \(791\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) and on a per residue basis a \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) value of \(10.9\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\). Likewise, the average value for \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has a value of \(862\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) and on a per residue basis a \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) value of \(12.0\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\).
The heat capacity change for unfolding reflects the difference in solvent exposure between the folded and unfolded states of a protein (Murphy & Freire, 1992; Privalov, 1992). Small, single domain naturally occurring proteins have \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) values of \(10{-}14\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\) (Kuwajima, 1989; Pittsyn et al., 1990). Molten globule proteins have low \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) values less than \(4\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\) (Freire, 1995). The \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) values for \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) with values of \(10.8\), \(10.9\), and \(12.0\;\mathrm{cal\;mol^{-1}\;K^{-1}}\) residue\(^{-1}\) are similar to natural proteins.
## NMR spectroscopy
The NMR spectra of natural proteins show well-dispersed chemical shifts with narrow line-widths in both the amide and aliphatic regions (Wüthrich, 1986). These features arise from a unique wellfolded native state. The \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) one-dimensional NMR spectra of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) are all well-dispersed in both amide and aliphatic regions similar to natural proteins. The \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)resonances for all the variants are shifted upfield to \(3.8\) to \(4.3\;\mathrm{ppm}\) indicative of \(\alpha\)-helical conformation (Wishart et al., 1995; Wüthrich, 1986). \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has a well-dispersed amide region but the aliphatic region shows less chemical shift dispersion relative to the spectra of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (data not shown).
Figure 4 shows the \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) heteronuclear single quantum coherence (HSQC) spectra of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\). The line-widths are sharp in both the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) and \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) dimensions typical of a protein with \(73\) amino acid residues. There are resonances of \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) and \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) that overlay well with the \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), indicating amide chemical shifts that are not affected by the introduction of the larger side-chains into the hydrophobic core. Moreover, there are also chemical shift differences between the two core variants and \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), indicating a backbone rearrangement taking place with the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) and \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) nuclei seeing different chemical environments.
chemical shifts indicate a well-ordered backbone environment for all variants.
Further insights into the hydrophobic interiors of \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \), A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \), and A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) was investigated by collecting natural abundance \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectra
(Figure 5). The \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectra show the methyl groups of isoleucine, leucine, and valine residues (Figure 5). Figure 5(b), shows the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectrum of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) (in red) overlaid onto the \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) spectrum (in black). The three isoleucine \( \delta \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) are well resolved in both the \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) dimensions indicating a unique chemical environment (Figure 5(b)). However, the chemical shift dispersion of the three isoleucine \( \gamma \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) are collapsed in both the \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) dimension (Figure 5b). The A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) \( \gamma \) methyl groups of V53 are well resolved similar to \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \). \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) has seven leucine residues and the \( 14\,\delta \) methyl groups are easily accounted for in the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectrum (Figure 5(a)). On the contrary, the 16 leucine \( \delta \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) show significant overlap (Figure 5(b)). The \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) chemical shift dispersion of \( \sim0.45 \) ppm for the leucine \( \delta \)s of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) (Figure 5(b)) is less than \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) of \( \sim0.60 \) ppm (Figure 5(b)). There is also less \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift dispersion for A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) relative to \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) (Figure 5(a) and (b)).
In Figure 5(c), the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectrum of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) is overlaid onto the \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) spectrum. The \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectrum of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) (Figure 5(c)) shows much less deterioration of \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift dispersion than A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) (Figure 5(b)), but not as much dispersion as the \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \)-HSQC spectrum (Figure 5(a)). The four isoleucine \( \delta \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) are discernable in the spectrum; however, the chemical shifts of two \( \delta \) methyl groups overlap in both the \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) dimension (Figure 5(c)). The other two isoleucine \( \delta \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) overlap in the \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) dimension (Figure 5(c)). Two of the \( \gamma \) methyl groups of isoleucine of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) overlay well with the \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) spectrum (Figure 5(c)). The other isoleucine \( \gamma \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) share similar chemical environments indicated by the overlapping \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shifts (Figure 5(c)). Once again, the V53 \( \gamma \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) are well resolved (Figure 5(c)). The leucine \( \delta \) methyl groups of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) show a decrease in both \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) chemical shift dispersion (Figure 5(c)). Ten \( \delta \) methyl groups are easily discerned in the of A60L- \( \alpha_{3}\mathrm{D} \) spectrum with the remainder overlapping in the region around 0.75 \( (^{1}\mathrm{H}) \) and 25 \( (^{13}\mathrm{C}) \) ppm (Figure 5(c)).
## Discussion
Others have used mutations to the cores of natural proteins to examine the driving force for protein folding and stabilization (Kellis et al., 1988; Lim & Sauer, 1991; Matthews, 1995; Richards & Lim, 1993; Shortle, 1992). One type of mutation, designated small-to-large mutations involved changing small, buried side-chains to a larger apolar residue. Although the increase in the bulk of the side-chain increases the hydrophobic driving force for folding, such substitutions were almost always found to be highly destabilizing (Liu et al., 2000).
The destabilization presumably is a result of steric clashes, leading to strain in the protein structure. In general, Ala-to-Leu or Ala-to-Ile substitutions are destabilizing by approximately 1.5 to \(4.5\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) (Liu et al., 2000). These studies clearly show that natural proteins have limited flexibility, and can accommodate the introduction of a larger sidechain, but only at an energetic penalty. A second type of mutation used to examine protein stability is a large-to-small mutation, in which a large, apolar side-chain in the core is mutated to a smaller residue. Mutations of either Leu or Ile to an Ala residue are generally destabilizing by approximately 2 to \(5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\) (Matthews, 1995). Matthews and coworkers have demonstrated that the destabilization arises from two effects: first is the decreased hydrophobicity of the side-chain, and the second involves the introduction of a cavity in the protein core. Depending on the site, the protein matrix can relax to fill the cavity, attenuating the destabilizing effect of the mutation. Therefore, mutations at highly flexible sites are less destabilizing (on the order of \(2\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\)), while more rigid sites show greater degrees of destabilization (reaching values of \(5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\) for sites that result in large cavities) (Xu et al., 1998).
The mutations into \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) can be considered as either small-to-large or large-to-small depending on the frame of reference. \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has an Ala residue at position 60, and examination of the solution structure shows no appearance of a cavity in this region of the protein. Therefore, the finding that A60 can be changed to Leu or Ile residue with a small increase in stability indicates that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) is more malleable than most natural proteins. Therefore, the increase in volume is not only tolerated, but has actually stabilized the protein due to the hydrophobic effect. It is interesting that the spectral properties of the mutants are distinct; \(\mathrm{A60L-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) shows a large decrease in dispersion in both the backbone amide \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) and \(^{15}\mathrm{N}\) resonances (Figure 4(a)) as well as many of the side-chain methyl groups (Figure 5(b)). By contrast, \(\mathrm{A60I-}\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) shows slightly better dispersion of the backbone amide \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) and \(^{15}\mathrm{N}\) resonances (Figure 4(b)), and a less severe erosion in the dispersion of the sidechain methyl resonances (Figure 5(c)).
Alternatively, it is possible to consider these mutations with respect to the starting Coil-Ser peptide (Lovejoy et al., 1993), which had a Leu sidechain at a position that became A60 in \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (Bryson et al., 1998). In this context, it is noteworthy that the re-packing algorithm used to define the sequence of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) predicted that a Leu or Ile residue would also be stable at this position in the sequence (Bryson et al., 1998). Therefore, it is equally valid to consider either A60L or A60I as the parent protein, and \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) as a large-to-small mutant of these structures. Viewed in this light, the finding that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) is only approximately \(1.5\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) less stable than A60L or A60I is highly significant. This finding would again indicate that position 60 is highly flexible and able to accommodate the destabilization presumably a result of steric clashes, leading to strain in the protein structure. In general, Ala-to-Leu or Ala-to-Ile substitutions are destabilizing by approximately 1.5 to \(4.5\;\mathrm{kcal}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) (Liu et al., 2000). These studies clearly show that natural proteins have limited flexibility, and can accommodate the introduction of a larger sidechain, but only at an energetic penalty. A second type of mutation used to examine protein stability is a large-to-small mutation, in which a large, apolar side-chain in the core is mutated to a smaller residue. Mutations of either Leu or Ile to an Ala residue are generally destabilizing by approximately 2 to \(5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\) (Matthews, 1995). Matthews and coworkers have demonstrated that the destabilization arises from two effects: first is the decreased hydrophobicity of the side-chain, and the second involves the introduction of a cavity in the protein core. Depending on the site, the protein matrix can relax to fill the cavity, attenuating the destabilizing effect of the mutation. Therefore, mutations at highly flexible sites are less destabilizing (on the order of \(2\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\)), while more rigid sites show greater degrees of destabilization (reaching values of \(5\;\mathrm{kcal\;mol^{-1}}\) for sites that result in large cavities) (Xu et al., 1998).
