The hydrophobic side-chains in \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) cluster into six distinct layers, each containing three residues (Figure 6). Phe38 is in the same layer as A60 (Figure 6). The aromatic ring of F38 lies proximal to the side-chains of L42, L56, I35, and I14, in adjacent layers (Figure 6). As a result many of these side-chains show ring current chemical shifts effects, with the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) chemical shift of the \(\delta\) methyl groups of L42 being particularly strongly shifted upfield (Figure 5(a)). In A60L- \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), these resonances loose their strongly perturbed chemical shift (Figure 5(b)). Although we have not conducted sequence-specific assignments of these mutants, it is nevertheless apparent that the \(\delta\) and \(\gamma\) methyl resonances of I14 and I35 move towards random coil values (Wishart et al., 1995). In a similar way, the methyl group chemical shifts of L42 and L56 move towards random coil values (Wishart et al., 1995). It is most interesting that even the \(\gamma\) methyl chemical shifts of the single Val residue, V53, which is two layers away from F38, shifts towards random coil values (Wishart et al., 1995). These data are most consistent with perturbation to the packing associated with the small-to-large mutation. Because V53 is affected, and all of the changes involve movements towards a less well-dispersed spectrum, the overall structure A60L- \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) may be more mobile than \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\).
A somewhat different picture emerges from a comparison of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) with A60L- \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\). First, the \(\gamma\) methyl resonances of V53 are almost unaffected (Figure 5(c)), suggesting that the effects of the
mutation may be more local. Also, the \(\gamma\) methyl region of the Ile residues remains well-dispersed (Figure 5(c)), suggesting that the I14, I35, and I63 layer of side-chains (Figure 6) may be unaffected. However, there is a loss of resolution in the Leu methyl region (Figure 5(c)). Once again, the \(\delta\) methyl groups of L42 and L56 move closer to random coil values (Wishart et al., 1995). Therefore, the mutation of A60 to Ile has affected the packing primarily of the F7, L42, and L56 layer (Figure 6). Whether the change in chemical shifts is a result of more dynamic behavior or simply an alteration in the conformation of F38 remains to be determined
Taken together, these experiments suggest that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has a slightly more flexible structure as compared to typical, natively folded natural proteins. This concept is described schematically in Figure 7, in which the energetic consequences of a small changes in the nuclear coordinates of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) is compared to the corresponding displacements of natural, native structures, molten globules, and unfolded states. For convenience, the minima of these curves are scaled to be equal. At one extreme, the unfolded state is very flexible, and hence the displacement/energy curve is nearly flat. At the other extreme a natural protein lies within a very steep energy well. We hypothesize that \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has an energy well whose steepness is intermediate between that of a native structure and a molten globule.
It should be emphasized that only a few natural proteins have been examined at the same level of detail as \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\), using thermodynamics, mutational analysis, and NMR relaxation of the main-chain atoms as well as side-chain methyl groups, not shown here. It is interesting to view \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) as a
relatively primitive model protein. Its structure lacks well-defined hydrogen bonded interactions between residues distance in sequence, as almost universally observed in natural proteins. (Solvent-exposed electrostatic interactions may destabilize alternatively folded states, but most probably do not stabilize the native structure of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) (Lumb & Kim, 1995)). It is interesting that such long-range, buried hydrogen-bonded interactions have been shown to be important for maintaining the uniquely packed core of \(\alpha_{2}\mathrm{D}\) (Hill et al., 2000), a dimeric four-helix bundle protein, whose solution structure has been solved to very high resolution (Hill & DeGrado, 1998). Further, \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) has been designed only to fold, and it does not have a binding or catalytic function. Therefore, it has not been subjected to any functional selection that might require precise definition of the conformation of specific side-chains or the orientations of secondary structural elements. We are presently using a combination of computational and phage display methods to evolve binding activity into \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\). It will be interesting to determine whether the acquisition of the function will affect its dynamic properties.
## Materials and Methods
## Expression and purification
The expression of \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) was produced behind an inducible T7 promoter in pET-15b (Novagen). Point mutations of A60 to a leucine and isoleucine were introduced using the Quick-change site-directed mutagenesis protocol (Stratagene). Mutations were confirmed by DNA sequencing. All three protein variants were purified as described by Walsh et al. (1999). Uniformly \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\)-labeled proteins were produced by growing the Escherichia coli cells in M9 minimal media supplemented with \({}^{15}\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}\) (1 g/l, Isotec) as the sole nitrogen source.
## Analytical ultracentrifugation
Sedimentation equilibrium experiments were conducted on an Beckman XL-I instrument equipped with both absorbance and interference optics. Experiments were run with protein concentrations of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) in \(10~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}~5.0)\) and \(100~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium chloride at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Equilibrium experiments were run at three speeds of 30,000, 35,000 and \(40,000~\mathrm{rpm}\). Equilibrium was assumed when successive radial scans at the same speeds were identical. The sedimentation equilibrium can be described by the following equation:
\[
M_{\mathrm{p}}=M_{\mathrm{w}}(1-\bar{\nu}\mathrm{p})
\]
where \(M_{\mathrm{p}}\) is the measured buoyant molecular mass, \(M_{\mathrm{w}}\) is the molecular mass in daltons, \(\nu\) is the partial specific volume of the solute, and \(\uprho\) is the density of the sample solution. The partial specific volume was calculated for each \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) variant using the weighted averages of the amino acid content (Kharakoz, 1997). Equilibrium curves were analyzed using the program IGOR Pro (v. 3.14, Wavemetrics) using a modified global fitting routine written by Jim Lear.
## Fluorescence spectroscopy
Fluorescence measurements were carried out on a phase-modulating SLM-4800 spectrofluorimeter (SLM-Aminco). Fluorescence intensities, phase/modulation lifetimes, and polarizations were made using multi-pass cuvettes. The signal was averaged for 30 seconds for each point using monochromator slits ranging from 4 to \(8~\mathrm{nm}\). Lifetime measurements employed a \(30~\mathrm{MHz}\) modulated frequency. Polarization measurements used Glan-Tompson prisms. The polarization and lifetime data were the average of four independent measurements.
## CD spectroscopy
CD spectra were recorded on a Aviv 62A DS spectropolarimeter (Aviv Associates, Lakewood, NJ). Protein concentrations were determined by measuring the absorbance at \(280~\mathrm{nm}\) in \(6~\mathrm{M}\) guanidine hydrochloride using a extinction coefficient at \(280~\mathrm{nm}\) of \(8300~\mathrm{M}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Pace
et al., 1995). Mean residue ellipticities were calculated using the equation:
\[
[\theta]=\theta_{\mathrm{obs}}/10\ln
\]
where \(\theta_{\mathrm{obs}}\) is the ellipticity measured in millidegrees, \(l\) is the length of the cell in centimeters, \(c\) is the concentration in \(\mathbf{M}\), and \(n\) is the number of residues.
Guanidine hydrochloride denaturations were run using a Hamilton dual-syringe titrator (Aviv Associates) at \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The loss of ellipticity at \(222\;\mathrm{nm}\) was used to monitor the chemical denaturation using a stirred-1 cm cell. Protein concentrations were \(25~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) in \(10~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}\,5.0)\). Assuming a two-state denaturation unfolding transition \((\mathbf{N}\leftrightarrow\mathbf{U})\), baseline corrected curves were converted to fraction unfolded \((f_{\mathrm{u}})\) as described by Agashe & Udgaonkar (1995), Santoro & Bolen (1988, 1992). The equilibrium constant \((K_{\mathrm{app}})\) is related to the \(f_{\mathrm{u}}\) by \(K_{\mathrm{app}}\!=\!f_{\mathrm{u}}/(1\!-\!f_{\mathrm{u}})\). The fraction unfolded curves versus GdmCl concentration were fit by a non-linear least squares method described to the equation by Agashe & Udgaonkar (1995), Santoro & Bolen, 1988, 1992).
GdmCl dependent thermal denaturations were collected in \(0.1\;\mathrm{cm}\) cells by monitoring the loss of ellipticity at \(222\;\mathrm{nm}\) using a five-sample cell changer. Samples were run with protein concentrations of \({\sim}20~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) protein in \(25~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium phosphate and \(100~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium chloride \((\mathrm{pH}\ 7.3)\). Thermal denaturations were monitored from 2 to \(94\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by \(2^{\circ}\) increments using a circulating water bath. Samples were equilibrated for four minutes at each time point and the signal was averaged for 30 seconds. GdmCl dependent thermal denaturations were fit directly to the Gibbs-Helmoltz equation as described by Agashe & Udgaonkar (1995), Chen & Schellman (1989). The thermal denaturation curves were fit simultaneously using the global fitting algorithm in IGOR Pro (v. 3.14, Wavemetrics). Every parameter was fit locally except for \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) which was globally fit.
A van't Hoff analysis of the GdmCl dependent thermal denaturations was also used to determine \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\). The van't Hoff enthalpies, \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{yH}}\) were determined from the slope of a plot of \(R\ast\ln K_{\mathrm{app}}\) versus \(T^{-1}\) evaluated near the thermal transition midpoint. The \(\Delta C_{\mathrm{p}}\) parameter was determined from the slope of the linear plot of \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{yH}}\) versus \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\) (Agashe & Udgaonkar, 1995; Santoro & Bolen, 1992).
## NMR spectroscopy
NMR spectra were collected on a Varian Inova 500 spectrometer equipped with a triple axis pulsed field gradient triple resonance probe. All \(\alpha_{3}\mathrm{D}\) variants were \(2\;\mathrm{mM}\) in protein concentration in \(50\;\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{d}_{3}\)-sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}\ 5.0)\), \(0.05\%\) \((\mathrm{w/v})\) sodium azide, and \(92{:}8\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{:}^{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). All spectra were run at \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HSQC}\) and natural abundance \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HSQC}\) spectra were run as described by Cavanagh et al. (1996). The \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HSQC}\) spectra were run with 64 complex point in the \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) dimension and 1024 complex points in the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) dimensions with 16 scans per transient. The \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) carriers were placed at \(116~\mathrm{ppm}\) and \(4.7~\mathrm{ppm}\), respectively. The spectral widths for the \({}^{15}\mathrm{N}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) dimensions were \(1700~\mathrm{Hz}\) and \(7000~\mathrm{Hz}\), respectively. The natural abundance \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HSQC}\) spectra were run with 80 complex points in the \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) dimension and 1024 complex points in the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) dimension with 256 scans per transient. The \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) carriers were placed at \(30~\mathrm{ppm}\) and \(4.7~\mathrm{ppm}\), respectively. The spectral widths for the \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) dimensions were \(5052~\mathrm{Hz}\) and \(7000~\mathrm{Hz}\), respectively. All data were processed and analyzed using the FELIX package (Biosym Technologies).
